# Debug Puppet Installation - Lab Walkthrough

This repository documents the troubleshooting and resolution of a misbehaving Puppet profile configuration during the **[Debug Puppet Installation](https://www.skills.google/focuses/50000183?parent=lti_session)** lab on Google Skills.

## Scenario & Objective
A custom Puppet script under the production environment (`profile` module) was completely overwriting the system `$PATH` environment variable instead of appending to it, breaking standard execution binaries like `ls`, `cd`, and `rm`. The objective was to debug the manifest file (`init.pp`), correct the `$PATH` logic, secure file permissions, and verify automation execution using the Puppet agent.

---

## Technical Troubleshooting & Resolution

### 1. Initial Access & PATH Restoration
Upon SSH connection, core binaries were inaccessible due to an incomplete `$PATH`. The path was temporarily restored to execute administrative commands:
```bash
export PATH=/bin:/usr/bin
2. Manifest Inspection
Navigated to the Puppet production module directory and inspected the faulty class definition:

Bash
cd /etc/puppet/code/environments/production/modules/profile/manifests
cat init.pp
Original Faulty Configuration (init.pp):

Puppet
class profile {
        file { '/etc/profile.d/append-path.sh':
                owner   => 'root',
                group   => 'root',
                mode    => '0646',
                content => "PATH=/java/bin\n",
        }
}
3. Implementing the Fix
Updated init.pp to:

Securely append /java/bin to the existing shell path using an escaped variable reference (\$PATH:/java/bin).

Harden file permissions from an insecure 0646 to 0644 (restricting write access solely to the root user).

Corrected Configuration (init.pp):

Puppet
class profile {
        file { '/etc/profile.d/append-path.sh':
                owner   => 'root',
                group   => 'root',
                mode    => '0644',
                content => "PATH=\$PATH:/java/bin\n",
        }
}
4. Applying and Verifying Configuration
Triggered a manual local run of the Puppet agent to compile and enforce the catalog:

Bash
sudo puppet agent -v --test
Verified the environment variable successfully loaded all system paths alongside /java/bin in a secondary session:

Bash
echo $PATH
Expected Output:

Plaintext
/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/games:/usr/local/games:/snap/bin:/java/bin
Lab Metrics & Completion
Score: 5 / 5 (100%)

Status: Verified and Completed Successfully.
EOF

echo "[*] Creating automated remediation script (fix_puppet.sh)..."
cat << 'EOF' > fix_puppet.sh
#!/bin/bash

Automation Script for Debug Puppet Installation Lab
export PATH=/bin:/usr/bin
cd /etc/puppet/code/environments/production/modules/profile/manifests

sudo tee init.pp > /dev/null << 'INNER_EOF'
class profile {
file { '/etc/profile.d/append-path.sh':
owner   => 'root',
group   => 'root',
mode    => '0644',
content => "PATH=$PATH:/java/bin\n",
}
}
INNER_EOF

sudo puppet agent -v --test
echo "[*] Puppet configuration applied successfully."
EOF

chmod +x fix_puppet.sh

echo "[*] Setup complete! README.md and fix_puppet.sh have been generated successfully."
